</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS5.4" class="ltx_text">IV-E</span> </span><span id="S4.SS5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Placer: Configuration Selection</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">MaaSO partitions the GPU cluster into specialized sub-clusters and determines optimal instance configurations for each sub-cluster, as illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ IV-A Overview ‣ IV MaaSO Design ‣ MaaSO: SLO-aware Orchestration of Heterogeneous Model Instances for MaaS" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. The placer employs two algorithms to realize such optimality. Alg. <a href="#alg1" title="In IV-D Placer: Configuration Scoring ‣ IV MaaSO Design ‣ MaaSO: SLO-aware Orchestration of Heterogeneous Model Instances for MaaS" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> performs simulator-guided instance deployment for an individual GPU cluster, while Alg. <a href="#alg2" title="In IV-D Placer: Configuration Scoring ‣ IV MaaSO Design ‣ MaaSO: SLO-aware Orchestration of Heterogeneous Model Instances for MaaS" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> implements dynamic programming-based resource partitioning and coordinates configurations across sub-clusters, optimizing the overall service performance based on global serving score.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce the two algorithms using a two-sub-cluster example: one serving throughput-oriented requests <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> with strict deadlines, and another serving latency-tolerant requests <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> with relaxed SLO constraints (e.g., green boxed requests in Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ III-B Impact of Inference Batch size ‣ III Performance Analysis of LLM Configuration ‣ MaaSO: SLO-aware Orchestration of Heterogeneous Model Instances for MaaS" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>-(b)). This example of sub-cluster partitioning is sufficient to demonstrate workload distribution for diverse request SLOs. When more sub-clusters are needed, we can scale to orchestrate multiple sub-clusters via the same dynamic programming approach in Alg. <a href="#alg2" title="In IV-D Placer: Configuration Scoring ‣ IV MaaSO Design ‣ MaaSO: SLO-aware Orchestration of Heterogeneous Model Instances for MaaS" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Alg. <a href="#alg1" title="In IV-D Placer: Configuration Scoring ‣ IV MaaSO Design ‣ MaaSO: SLO-aware Orchestration of Heterogeneous Model Instances for MaaS" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> iteratively configures model instances for any sub-cluster <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> using a greedy heuristic. After selecting candidate configurations through pruning strategies (line 4), the algorithm employs a nested loop structure to evaluate each configuration. For each configuration <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{P}_{i},\mathcal{B}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{P}_{i},\mathcal{B}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, the placer identifies the model <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with the largest number of unserved requests from the remaining candidate set <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\setminus M_{sat}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\setminus M_{sat}</annotation></semantics></math> and creates a new deployment <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> by adding an instance of <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> (lines 9-11). If the new instance improves the serving score <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>, it is accepted; otherwise, model <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is added to the saturated set <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{sat}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{sat}</annotation></semantics></math> to prevent further unproductive exploration (lines 13-16). The algorithm maintains optimal solutions <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}^{*}[k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}^{*}[k]</annotation></semantics></math> and corresponding serving scores <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{*}[k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{*}[k]</annotation></semantics></math> for all possible GPU allocations <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> across all evaluated configurations (lines 17-19).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Alg. <a href="#alg2" title="In IV-D Placer: Configuration Scoring ‣ IV MaaSO Design ‣ MaaSO: SLO-aware Orchestration of Heterogeneous Model Instances for MaaS" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> coordinates sub-cluster resource allocation by partitioning the GPU cluster <math id="S4.SS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> into specialized sub-clusters <math id="S4.SS5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[G_{t},G_{l}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[G_{t},G_{l}]</annotation></semantics></math> and determining optimal instances configurations <math id="S4.SS5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I^{t},I^{l}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I^{t},I^{l}]</annotation></semantics></math> for each sub-cluster. The algorithm first categorizes requests by SLO requirements (line 3), separating throughput-oriented requests <math id="S4.SS5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> from latency-tolerant requests <math id="S4.SS5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since serving requests with stricter deadlines typically requires higher-degree tensor parallelism or reduced inference batch size, the algorithm employs an asymmetric resource allocation strategy: it provides initial estimation only for sub-cluster <math id="S4.SS5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> based on request proportions (line 5), while allowing <math id="S4.SS5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> access to all GPUs during solution generation (line 8).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Then, Alg. <a href="#alg1" title="In IV-D Placer: Configuration Scoring ‣ IV MaaSO Design ‣ MaaSO: SLO-aware Orchestration of Heterogeneous Model Instances for MaaS" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is invoked for both sub-clusters to obtain solution arrays with optimal configurations. The dynamic programming phase (lines 11-16) explores all feasible resource partitions <math id="S4.SS5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g_{t},g_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g_{t},g_{l})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.SS5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}+g_{l}=|G|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}+g_{l}=|G|</annotation></semantics></math>, accessing pre-computed serving scores <math id="S4.SS5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\phi_{t},\phi_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\phi_{t},\phi_{l})</annotation></semantics></math> and maximizing their summation to determine the optimal partition. To ensure robustness, the algorithm initializes <math id="S4.SS5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{opt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{opt}</annotation></semantics></math> with a homogeneous deployment baseline (line 10), allowing it to revert to homogeneous strategies when heterogeneous deployment provides no performance gains. This would bring extra orchestration overhead, while simple zero initialization would be sufficient in most cases. In extreme cases, heterogeneous instance scheme may not yield performance gains. We will discuss these boundary conditions and their impact in the limitations analysis of Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="V-B Results and Analysis ‣ V Experiments ‣ MaaSO: SLO-aware Orchestration of Heterogeneous Model Instances for MaaS" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The decomposition of these two algorithms offers significant computational advantages over joint optimization approaches. The overall complexity is dominated by Alg. <a href="#alg1" title="In IV-D Placer: Configuration Scoring ‣ IV MaaSO Design ‣ MaaSO: SLO-aware Orchestration of Heterogeneous Model Instances for MaaS" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>: The pruning strategies reduce the search space from <math id="S4.SS5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(|\mathcal{P}|\times|\mathcal{B}|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(|\mathcal{P}|\times|\mathcal{B}|)</annotation></semantics></math> to approximately <math id="S4.SS5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(|\mathcal{P}|\times|\mathcal{B}|_{\text{valid}}/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mtext>valid</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(|\mathcal{P}|\times|\mathcal{B}|_{\text{valid}}/2)</annotation></semantics></math>, where the ratio of <math id="S4.SS5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{B}|_{\text{valid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mtext>valid</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{B}|_{\text{valid}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{B}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{B}|</annotation></semantics></math> depends on the difference in SLO demand between the full request set <math id="S4.SS5.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> and sub-set <math id="S4.SS5.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. The dynamic programming adds only <math id="S4.SS5.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(|G|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(|G|)</annotation></semantics></math> operations, to enable complete resource allocation space exploration. By pre-computing all possible deployment solutions, we avoid the exponential complexity of joint optimization.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS6.4" class="ltx_text">IV-F</span> </span><span id="S4.SS6.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Distributor: Requests Distribution</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As detailed in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="III-B Impact of Inference Batch size ‣ III Performance Analysis of LLM Configuration ‣ MaaSO: SLO-aware Orchestration of Heterogeneous Model Instances for MaaS" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-B</span></a>, the workload distribution policy should prevent requests from experiencing cascaded timeout due to improper instance assignments. The distributor enforces SLO-aware distribution through a three-step workflow:</p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sub-cluster mapping</span>: Classify requests by SLO requirements into <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>, then route them to corresponding sub-clusters <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> established by the placer.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Instance assignment</span>: For active instances within the target sub-cluster, evaluate each instance’s capability to meet the request’s SLO and select the instance with the shortest request queue to achieve load balancing.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overflow protection</span>: Block request <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> assignment when <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{q}^{r}+L_{d}^{r}&gt;\tau_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{q}^{r}+L_{d}^{r}&gt;\tau_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is predicted, where <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{d}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{d}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is estimated using worst-case instance throughput <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(M,P,\mathcal{B}_{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(M,P,\mathcal{B}_{I})</annotation></semantics></math>, so as to prevent cascaded timeouts caused by optimistic estimation.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This distributor workflow is illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ IV-A Overview ‣ IV MaaSO Design ‣ MaaSO: SLO-aware Orchestration of Heterogeneous Model Instances for MaaS" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> using the two-sub-cluster example from Section <a href="#S4.SS5" title="IV-E Placer: Configuration Selection ‣ IV MaaSO Design ‣ MaaSO: SLO-aware Orchestration of Heterogeneous Model Instances for MaaS" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-E</span></a>. For example, request <math id="S4.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{1}_{a}\in R_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{1}_{a}\in R_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for model <math id="S4.SS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> is routed to instance <math id="S4.SS6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{t}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{t}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. The conservative estimation ensures that <math id="S4.SS6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{r}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{r}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> prediction preserves time margins, preventing the propagation of cascaded timeouts during continuous batching.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experiments</span></h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">V-A</span> </span><span id="S5.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Experimental Setting</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hardware Environment.</span> Our experiments utilize two servers: (1) a server equipped with two Intel Xeon E5-2680 CPUs, 128GB of memory, two NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3090 GPUs (24GB each), and (2) a server equipped with two Intel Xeon Platinum 8468V CPUs, 2048GB of memory, eight NVIDIA HGX H20 GPUs (96GB each). All LLM instances employ vLLM as the inference engine with bfloat16 quantization. Since conducting experiments on a MaaS production cluster is prohibitively expensive, we perform cluster-scale simulation experiments using profiling data from our own servers. The simulated cluster is composed of multiple AWS EC2 p3.16xlarge instances<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, each equipped with 8 NVIDIA Tesla V100 GPUs (16GB each).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE I: </span>Experimental Trace Configurations</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Trace</span></th>
<td id="S5.T1.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decode Length</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SLO Factor</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" rowspan="2"><span id="S5.T1.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proportion</span></td></tr>
